0
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Downloaded From www.AllSubs.org

0
00:00:00,105 --> 00:00:04,996
Shared by http://DJJ.HOME.SAPO.PT/

1
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:17,451
- I'll get the tip.
- Thank you.

2
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,417
- Thanks, lad.
- Keep well.

3
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,979
- Cheerio.
- Bye.

4
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,251
Put the cleaver down, mate.
Everybody calm down, all right?

5
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:02,969
If you all want to chance it,
I'll tell you now, I've got a full house.

6
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:07,531
All in, right?

7
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,575
- You dropped this.
- Thank you very much.

8
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,218
Can I buy you a drink?

9
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:20,860
- All right, sure. Why not?
- What'll you have?

10
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:24,130
- Scotch, thanks very much.
- Scotch.

11
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:35,175
All right!

12
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:14,609
Busy?

13
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:45,292
Ash. There are easier ways
to earn a living.

14
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:55,095
Mickey.

15
00:04:57,220 --> 00:04:58,619
How good!

16
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:00,976
Have I missed you or what?

17
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:05,174
- How's life in the fast lane?
- It's good.

18
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,692
It's the only game in town.
If they're not eating a hamburger...

19
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:17,340
or fiddling with the CD player,
they're on the phone.

20
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:19,650
No one watches the road anymore.

21
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:20,729
Cheers, Eddie.

22
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:24,135
So what about you? How was it?

23
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:28,574
A new experience.
One I don't care to repeat.

24
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,378
So what now then?

25
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:16,010
I feel like one last score.

26
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:17,613
- Long or short?
- Long.

27
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:19,577
No one is playing the long con anymore.

28
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:23,369
They don't because they can't.
Come on, one last time.

29
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:27,215
Then I'll stop. I'll retire on my terms.
Not because I've been beat.

30
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:36,134
You know I can't do it without you.

31
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:56,609
- When do we start?
- Already have. Albert's got a mark.

32
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:59,294
But first things first.
Can you swing a hotel?

33
00:06:59,420 --> 00:07:01,138
Can Pavarotti sing?

34
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:23,217
We've already got Stone
under surveillance.

35
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:25,734
- I understand that, sir.
- Then why the heavy mob?

36
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:28,778
We've had intelligence
Stone's next target is well connected...

37
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:30,293
plays golf with the Chancellor.

38
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:34,419
So he's got friends in high places.
Why don't you just warn him off?

39
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,058
Whilst we are keen to protect him...

40
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:39,132
we're even more keen
to see Stone put away again.

41
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:41,211
- Have I got a choice?
- No.

42
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:46,532
All right, listen up.

43
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:50,454
This is Detective Inspector
Martin DePalma from Central.

44
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:53,852
He's here to oversee
the Mickey Stone surveillance.

45
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:55,578
Give him what he wants.

46
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:58,652
- Any overtime comes through me first.
- Sir.

47
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:07,614
Sgt. Terri Hodges.
I've been heading up the surveillance.

48
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:10,459
Good. Then let's see what you've got.

49
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:29,251
All right, cheers.

50
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:36,974
<i>Ashley Morgan.</i>

51
00:08:37,060 --> 00:08:39,699
Colloquially known as Ash
or "Three Socks. "

52
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:42,374
- Three Socks?
- He got the name in prison.

53
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:44,974
After his first visit to the shower block.

54
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:48,575
He's a fixer. Whatever you want,
Ash Morgan will find it.

55
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:51,413
He got a skull fracture in a barroom brawl
10 years ago.

56
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:53,650
He uses it to work the flop.

57
00:08:56,380 --> 00:08:59,099
Bounces over a car, goes to hospital...

58
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:02,138
x- ray shows the fracture,
insurance company pays out.

59
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:11,658
<i>Albert Stroller. Habitual gambler.</i>

60
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:16,450
Gee-gees, casinos, card games.
Albert plays them all. He's the roper.

61
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:19,930
He finds the marks, sucks them in,
and introduces them to the mob.

62
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:23,899
Usually plays the part of ex-serviceman
or retired banker.

63
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:27,450
In reality he was a shoe salesman
in the American Midwest.

64
00:09:27,540 --> 00:09:31,215
Legend has it, when his store closed down
and the staff made redundant...

65
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:35,532
he conned the company out of $60,000
and shared it amongst his coworkers.

66
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:37,292
He fled to England in the '70s...

67
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:40,531
but by then he'd got the taste
and hasn't looked back since.

68
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:42,576
Once Stroller's got his mark...

69
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:45,889
<i>it's time to move up to the next level.</i>

70
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:49,653
<i>The insideman.</i>

71
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:53,337
<i>Michael Stone.
Also known as "Mickey Bricks. "</i>

72
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:58,251
He's in a class all on his own.
A long con player. Never been convicted.

73
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:01,215
It is a matter of personal pride...

74
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:05,657
to every fraud investigator in the country,
to be the one who nails him.

75
00:10:05,740 --> 00:10:09,858
Just finished his first spell in prison for
an unrelated crime. He served two years.

76
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:14,172
Rumors are, this is his last score.
We miss him this time, it's game over.

77
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:16,649
We think this is their next mark.

78
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:18,899
Peter Reginald Williams...

79
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:21,699
started a pharmaceutical business
in the early '80s.

80
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:24,453
His first wife Sara was from old money.

81
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:28,658
He used her family's connections in
the government to get research contracts.

82
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:30,935
- Where's the in?
- He is greedy.

83
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:34,055
- I'll get the tip.
- Thank you.

84
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:37,052
<i>Regularly makes the Top 500 rich list.</i>

85
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:42,896
<i>But would cross the street
to pick up £1 coin.</i>

86
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:46,975
He recently bagged himself
a new young wife.

87
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:51,292
But she's high maintenance
and spends his cash faster than he'd like.

88
00:10:52,420 --> 00:10:55,173
He's generally a sucker for a pretty face.

89
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:59,856
Spends money to impress.
The shorter the skirt, the looser he gets.

90
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:04,535
Sounds like you really
warmed to this guy, Albert.

91
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:08,408
Yes. He's an objectionable little man,
with the morals of an alley cat.

92
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,411
That's marks for you.

93
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:16,332
This crew isn't about conning
little old ladies out of their pension.

94
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:18,650
They're strictly major league.

95
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:22,615
And remember, Mickey Bricks
thinks he's cleverer than us.

96
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:25,494
That there's no way we can catch him.

97
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:28,331
Let's prove him wrong.

98
00:11:35,540 --> 00:11:36,973
Time to get an edge.

99
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:56,770
- Did I miss something?
- One phone call in two years.

100
00:11:57,820 --> 00:12:00,254
Yeah, you know how I hate queuing.

101
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:04,370
I've got the crew together.

102
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:06,139
Everyone?

103
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:16,529
Albert?

104
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:20,333
We're on. Yeah, bring him in.

105
00:12:28,740 --> 00:12:31,334
Don't tip him, Tony.
That service was shite.

106
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:38,372
- You've dropped your wallet.
- It's not mine.

107
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:43,451
That's a lot of money.

108
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:49,329
Let's see. "Mark Hamilton.
Clacy Hughes Associates. "

109
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:52,689
What do you say? Finders keepers?

110
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:56,217
You are a card.
Let's see if he's staying here.

111
00:12:56,660 --> 00:13:00,130
- We found this on the floor.
- I'll see Mr. Hamilton gets it, sir.

112
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:03,656
No, I don't think so.
Just tell us which room he's in.

113
00:13:12,180 --> 00:13:14,978
- Who's she?
- Stacie Monroe.

114
00:13:15,580 --> 00:13:18,492
She was married to short con artist
Jake Henry.

115
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:21,458
They worked the badger mostly.
Did well, too.

116
00:13:21,540 --> 00:13:25,249
Then Jake took all their savings
and ran for the hills five years ago.

117
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:27,331
So much for honor amongst thieves.

118
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:29,570
Not to mention your wedding vows.

119
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:34,219
Word is, she and Stone had a thing once.
Before he met his wife.

120
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:38,931
Not just a pretty face. She's sharp.
Maybe even in Stone's league.

121
00:14:52,100 --> 00:14:53,374
Who is it?

122
00:14:53,460 --> 00:14:55,815
- Mr. Hamilton?
- Who wants to know?

123
00:14:55,940 --> 00:14:58,534
We think we found something of yours.

124
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:00,739
Your wallet.

125
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:12,094
£3.50.

126
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:17,088
I'll give you two gos to find the lady.

127
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:20,337
- Yeah, right. £3.50.
- Take that.

128
00:15:55,540 --> 00:15:57,849
Shouldn't you be somewhere, Danny?

129
00:15:57,940 --> 00:15:59,896
Tell you what, I'll make it easy for you.

130
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:02,858
I'll take the Queen, turn the corner up.
Like that.

131
00:16:06,540 --> 00:16:07,893
Here we go.

132
00:16:14,620 --> 00:16:18,329
Choose. No money.
If you get it wrong, just buy me a drink.

133
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:27,615
- Same again, please.
- You're a very sick person.

134
00:16:27,700 --> 00:16:29,531
Just naturally gifted.

135
00:16:31,220 --> 00:16:33,495
You heard Mickey Bricks is out?

136
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:38,056
- Plays the long con, right?
- Top of the food chain.

137
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:42,137
So what? You reckon
he could teach me something?

138
00:16:42,260 --> 00:16:45,411
What do I know?
All you guys freak me out.

139
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:48,174
But they say he's the best.

140
00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:16,934
He's booked in the London Park Hotel.

141
00:17:17,220 --> 00:17:19,370
- Yes, mate?
- Whiskey, please.

142
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:23,968
Thank you.

143
00:17:29,140 --> 00:17:30,778
Here's to you, gentlemen.

144
00:17:30,860 --> 00:17:33,772
I didn't think there were any
honest men left in the world.

145
00:17:33,860 --> 00:17:36,294
- One or two.
- Here's to honest men.

146
00:17:38,620 --> 00:17:42,898
- You must let me thank you properly.
- No. We wouldn't hear of it.

147
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:51,094
Even if it's only the cab fare home.

148
00:17:51,300 --> 00:17:52,619
Excuse me.

149
00:18:20,620 --> 00:18:22,053
Where were we?

150
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:26,418
I was trying to persuade you to take
something in return for your kindness.

151
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:30,334
It's a strange state of affairs
if one chap can't do a favor for another...

152
00:18:30,420 --> 00:18:32,411
without money rearing its ugly head.

153
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:36,334
One day you may be in a position
to do something similar for us.

154
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:40,095
You know, I just might.
I have a suggestion.

155
00:18:48,580 --> 00:18:53,051
A business associate has asked me
to make an investment for him tomorrow.

156
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:57,258
If you'll allow me,
I'll put in, say, £500 for you?

157
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:04,095
I'll take the £500 back,
you just get to keep the profit.

158
00:19:05,660 --> 00:19:07,059
It'll have cost me nothing...

159
00:19:07,140 --> 00:19:10,769
but at least I'll feel happier
knowing I've done something.

160
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:16,136
That sounds very generous.

161
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:18,175
Good.

162
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:33,379
Thank you.

163
00:19:33,460 --> 00:19:35,610
This investment, can I ask what it is?

164
00:19:35,700 --> 00:19:38,658
Just stocks and shares.
Nothing too exciting.

165
00:19:39,460 --> 00:19:40,813
Fingers crossed.

166
00:19:41,220 --> 00:19:43,575
I'm sure that won't be necessary.

167
00:19:52,860 --> 00:19:56,853
- What do you think?
- Looks like a no-lose situation to me.

168
00:19:56,940 --> 00:19:58,612
My favorite kind.

169
00:19:59,060 --> 00:20:01,051
They left about an hour ago.

170
00:20:01,140 --> 00:20:04,450
Stroller dropped Williams off,
then went back to the hotel.

171
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:16,056
Hi, Mickey. Nice to... I was just...
Okay, all right, take care.

172
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:19,371
Hi, Mickey. How're you doing? Danny.
I was just...

173
00:20:27,700 --> 00:20:29,691
This guy moves in high circles.

174
00:20:29,780 --> 00:20:32,010
It'll take something special
to impress him.

175
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:33,772
That's exactly what he'll get.

176
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:36,328
As soon as Ash starts working his magic.

177
00:20:36,420 --> 00:20:39,059
Have you ever thought of
a younger woman, Albie?

178
00:20:39,140 --> 00:20:42,018
My dear, at my age, I think of little else.

179
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:00,129
Mr. Stone? Hi. Danny Blue.

180
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:03,333
Pleased to meet you.
You've probably heard of me.

181
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:07,618
No? All right. I'm not gonna mess
you about. I'll come straight to the point.

182
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:10,498
I know you're playing a score. I want in.

183
00:21:10,860 --> 00:21:13,169
- In what?
- In. You know, in the game.

184
00:21:13,260 --> 00:21:17,458
I want to learn. I really want to learn.
I'll just come inside for a little chat.

185
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:19,098
This is nice, isn't it?

186
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:23,851
I'm good. I can help you.
Think of it like that. I'll do that for you.

187
00:21:23,940 --> 00:21:25,817
I know all the old plays.
What're you doing?

188
00:21:25,900 --> 00:21:28,858
The Wire? No? The Rag? The Pay-Off?

189
00:21:28,940 --> 00:21:31,215
You tell me once, that's it.

190
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:34,016
Bosh, it's in.

191
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:38,372
<i>I bet you watched The Sting, too,
didn't you?</i>

192
00:21:39,260 --> 00:21:40,978
Never heard of it.

193
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:46,018
- You're not gonna shut the door, are you?
- Yeah, I thought I might.

194
00:22:05,500 --> 00:22:07,855
So when do we pick them up?

195
00:22:09,380 --> 00:22:10,608
We wait.

196
00:22:11,540 --> 00:22:13,212
I want everything.

197
00:22:13,300 --> 00:22:16,576
All the players, the money.
Nothing left to chance.

198
00:22:16,660 --> 00:22:17,934
As far as we can make out...

199
00:22:18,020 --> 00:22:20,932
Mickey Bricks doesn't have the money
to bankroll a con like this.

200
00:22:28,540 --> 00:22:32,453
<i>They'll go out and try and raise
what they can with short cons.</i>

201
00:22:46,180 --> 00:22:49,331
<i>They'll need a car, top of the range.</i>

202
00:22:49,940 --> 00:22:53,933
<i>Not too flash,
but something that oozes class.</i>

203
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:02,659
Parking, sir?

204
00:23:08,020 --> 00:23:10,215
- Here.
- Thank you.

205
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:32,898
<i>God help anyone
who runs into them today.</i>

206
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:21,539
I'm sorry.

207
00:24:58,780 --> 00:25:00,099
<i>£240.</i>

208
00:25:01,220 --> 00:25:02,539
<i>£125.</i>

209
00:25:04,700 --> 00:25:05,735
Enough?

210
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:07,651
- What about this place?
- Sorted.

211
00:25:07,740 --> 00:25:09,093
Then it's enough.

212
00:25:54,740 --> 00:25:58,858
As promised, gentlemen,
I invested the £500 on your behalf...

213
00:25:58,940 --> 00:26:00,896
and this is the return.

214
00:26:01,900 --> 00:26:04,175
I hope you're not disappointed.

215
00:26:05,620 --> 00:26:07,611
There must be £1,000 here.

216
00:26:07,700 --> 00:26:10,578
£1,950, to be precise.

217
00:26:11,940 --> 00:26:14,659
- That's quite a return.
- I've had worse.

218
00:26:15,940 --> 00:26:18,818
Made quite a killing yourself, I'll wager.

219
00:26:19,100 --> 00:26:20,533
So shoot me.

220
00:26:20,740 --> 00:26:22,856
Forgive me for prying,
but I get the feeling...

221
00:26:22,940 --> 00:26:26,091
there was little or no risk
in this particular investment.

222
00:26:26,180 --> 00:26:28,011
No risk whatsoever.

223
00:26:29,340 --> 00:26:33,458
So, if you'll excuse me, gentlemen,
I do have a meeting.

224
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:37,010
Wait. I mean, why don't we go again?

225
00:26:39,700 --> 00:26:41,019
Again?

226
00:26:43,140 --> 00:26:46,177
Tony, Mr. Hamilton
has been more than generous.

227
00:26:48,260 --> 00:26:49,579
Thank you.

228
00:26:59,880 --> 00:27:01,871
- Can I help you?
- Love, is he in?

229
00:27:04,720 --> 00:27:06,790
- Excuse me.
- Good to see you again.

230
00:27:06,880 --> 00:27:08,552
Excuse me, gentlemen. How are you?

231
00:27:08,640 --> 00:27:12,155
Mr. Hamilton.
I want to shake you by the hand.

232
00:27:12,360 --> 00:27:16,751
You are a very great man,
and I'm sorry I ever doubted you.

233
00:27:17,320 --> 00:27:21,518
When I gave you that money, I did,
for one second, consider the possibility...

234
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:24,910
that you could have been, you know...
I can't even say it.

235
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:27,230
Go ahead. Please. Get it off your chest.

236
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:29,754
I thought you could have been
one of those conmen.

237
00:27:29,840 --> 00:27:32,070
I might never see my money or him
ever again.

238
00:27:43,160 --> 00:27:46,596
Unbelievable. I need a drink.
Scotch, please, darling.

239
00:27:46,880 --> 00:27:51,749
I'm sorry, gentlemen.
This is Mr. Redford, a client of mine.

240
00:27:51,920 --> 00:27:55,879
Mr. Redford, this is Tony Monroe
and Peter Williams.

241
00:27:55,960 --> 00:27:57,632
Peter, how do you do?

242
00:27:57,720 --> 00:28:00,598
I'm ready to go again,
but this time I wanna go bigger.

243
00:28:00,680 --> 00:28:03,672
Fine. But this is neither the time
nor the place to...

244
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:04,829
I'm ready.

245
00:28:06,240 --> 00:28:08,708
Perhaps you'd like to show
Tony and Peter out?

246
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:10,153
No, wait.

247
00:28:11,280 --> 00:28:15,398
If there's another deal up for grabs,
I'd like to hear about it.

248
00:28:17,080 --> 00:28:19,878
- Tony?
- Absolutely, yes.

249
00:28:23,560 --> 00:28:26,836
Thank you. My car will pick you up
tomorrow morning.

250
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:27,989
Thank you.

251
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:37,951
I'm no expert,
but I'd say that went like a dream.

252
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:48,635
- So am I in?
- You're in.

253
00:29:04,640 --> 00:29:05,709
Thank you.

254
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:52,269
Go.

255
00:32:22,040 --> 00:32:24,838
Just waiting for some new clients...

256
00:32:24,920 --> 00:32:27,388
here to see Mr. Hamilton in 105.

257
00:32:27,760 --> 00:32:30,752
- I'll check the list.
- They might not be on it.

258
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:33,189
If they're not on the list, I'll need to check.

259
00:32:39,040 --> 00:32:40,917
Isn't 105 Mr. Moss's office?

260
00:32:53,020 --> 00:32:55,488
- Do you believe in love at first sight?
- What?

261
00:32:56,100 --> 00:32:57,249
Don't panic.

262
00:32:59,540 --> 00:33:03,055
Do you know what it's like,
watching you sitting here every day?

263
00:33:03,140 --> 00:33:06,496
I just want an insy-winsy little kiss.
Can I, please?

264
00:33:07,420 --> 00:33:09,138
Mr. Hamilton's expecting you.

265
00:33:09,220 --> 00:33:10,972
- Right this way.
- Thank you.

266
00:33:29,340 --> 00:33:31,729
Got it. They've got a positive ID.

267
00:33:32,580 --> 00:33:35,140
He's a short con artist called Danny Blue.

268
00:33:35,340 --> 00:33:36,489
Any history?

269
00:33:36,620 --> 00:33:39,339
He played a con three months ago,
south of the river.

270
00:33:39,420 --> 00:33:41,092
But the mark withdrew his complaint...

271
00:33:41,180 --> 00:33:43,057
and CPS wouldn't prosecute
without evidence.

272
00:33:43,140 --> 00:33:44,698
Why did he pull out?

273
00:33:44,820 --> 00:33:47,334
The mark bet his life savings
in a horseracing scam...

274
00:33:47,420 --> 00:33:49,490
but his wife didn't know anything about it.

275
00:33:49,580 --> 00:33:51,616
I suppose he wanted to keep it that way.

276
00:33:51,700 --> 00:33:54,089
Hard to explain over breakfast, I guess.

277
00:33:54,980 --> 00:33:59,610
It must be true what they say:
"There's one born every minute. "

278
00:34:12,180 --> 00:34:14,740
Gentlemen, come in. Take a seat.

279
00:34:16,540 --> 00:34:17,609
Thank you.

280
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:19,819
Sorry I'm late, guys.

281
00:34:22,820 --> 00:34:24,458
Ran into an old friend.

282
00:34:26,220 --> 00:34:29,895
Gentlemen, welcome to the land of plenty.

283
00:34:31,220 --> 00:34:32,289
Thank you.

284
00:34:32,820 --> 00:34:35,778
What I'm about to show you
may be foolproof...

285
00:34:35,860 --> 00:34:37,691
but it's also illegal.

286
00:34:37,980 --> 00:34:40,448
There's no victim, but it's against the law.

287
00:34:40,540 --> 00:34:42,451
Would you like me to continue?

288
00:34:52,740 --> 00:34:55,254
You see, the first rule of the con...

289
00:34:55,740 --> 00:34:59,016
...is you can't cheat an honest man.
- It's never been done.

290
00:34:59,100 --> 00:35:00,818
- Can't happen.
- Impossible.

291
00:35:00,900 --> 00:35:03,494
It only works if you want something
for nothing.

292
00:35:03,580 --> 00:35:06,538
- What do we do?
- We give you nothing for something.

293
00:35:06,620 --> 00:35:08,292
You're £1,000 up so far.

294
00:35:08,380 --> 00:35:10,018
It's a very good time to walk away.

295
00:35:10,100 --> 00:35:12,409
- But he can't.
- Because he's so greedy.

296
00:35:12,700 --> 00:35:15,294
- So what do we do?
- Feed the greed.

297
00:35:16,100 --> 00:35:18,330
But he's got one last chance.

298
00:35:19,860 --> 00:35:21,612
We've told him it's illegal.

299
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:24,578
You could lose everything.

300
00:35:24,900 --> 00:35:28,688
- Career, wife, home.
- Prison.

301
00:35:29,180 --> 00:35:30,738
Get out while you can.

302
00:35:32,060 --> 00:35:33,857
Do the smart thing.

303
00:35:36,300 --> 00:35:38,973
As I was saying,
what I'm about to show you...

304
00:35:39,060 --> 00:35:41,494
may be foolproof, but it's against the law.

305
00:35:41,580 --> 00:35:43,696
Now, would you like me to continue?

306
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:50,130
Laws were made to be broken.

307
00:35:53,940 --> 00:35:56,977
Can you tell Peterson
I want those breakdowns by lunchtime?

308
00:35:57,460 --> 00:35:58,529
Thank you.

309
00:35:59,300 --> 00:36:01,734
Share transactions
from Tokyo and New York...

310
00:36:01,820 --> 00:36:04,288
are relayed to the city of London
by computer.

311
00:36:04,380 --> 00:36:07,417
Or more precisely, our computer.

312
00:36:07,500 --> 00:36:11,539
When the multi-nationals publish their
annual reports, there's a flurry of activity.

313
00:36:11,620 --> 00:36:15,249
If a company does well, their shares
attract buyers and the price jumps.

314
00:36:15,340 --> 00:36:19,015
We've managed to delay those
transactions in the system long enough...

315
00:36:19,100 --> 00:36:22,775
to assess them and put
our own order in to buy before them.

316
00:36:23,860 --> 00:36:26,215
So you buy, knowing the share prices
are gonna go up?

317
00:36:26,300 --> 00:36:28,450
- Exactly.
- They must notice the delay.

318
00:36:28,540 --> 00:36:30,656
Not when it's less than
nine-tenths of a second.

319
00:36:30,740 --> 00:36:32,332
You can do this in less than a second?

320
00:36:32,420 --> 00:36:35,890
Our mainframe can make three million
calculations in that time.

321
00:36:43,180 --> 00:36:44,818
What's in it for you?

322
00:36:45,340 --> 00:36:47,376
I take 25% Of your profit.

323
00:36:47,460 --> 00:36:49,690
<i>- 25%?
- Non-negotiable.</i>

324
00:36:49,860 --> 00:36:52,772
Hang on. The profit is guaranteed, right?

325
00:36:52,860 --> 00:36:56,535
Set in stone.
If there's no profit, I don't get paid.

326
00:36:58,140 --> 00:37:02,577
In three days, the oil company Vestron
are releasing their annual report.

327
00:37:02,700 --> 00:37:07,569
Once it's out, every major broker
will be crawling all over those shares.

328
00:37:07,860 --> 00:37:10,738
The plan is to buy
£500,000 worth of shares.

329
00:37:10,820 --> 00:37:13,653
I have two investors already in place
at £100,000 each.

330
00:37:13,740 --> 00:37:15,571
There are three places left.

331
00:37:16,020 --> 00:37:17,738
£100, 000?

332
00:37:19,180 --> 00:37:20,898
Great, yeah, I'm in.

333
00:37:22,700 --> 00:37:24,895
I don't know if I can raise
that kind of money...

334
00:37:24,980 --> 00:37:28,859
The boat leaves in three days.
Either you're on it, or you're not. Tony?

335
00:37:29,460 --> 00:37:32,372
It's a lot of money, but I'll find it.

336
00:37:41,320 --> 00:37:43,356
You don't mind if I call my broker?

337
00:37:43,440 --> 00:37:45,715
What I've just said can't leave this room.

338
00:37:45,800 --> 00:37:49,429
I'm not in the habit of investing
in companies I've never heard of.

339
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:57,676
George? Peter Williams.
What do you know about Vestron?

340
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:05,118
No problem.
Just heard a whisper, that's all.

341
00:38:06,360 --> 00:38:09,193
No. I'll call you if anything comes of it.

342
00:38:17,080 --> 00:38:18,513
Count me in.

343
00:38:19,640 --> 00:38:21,915
Good. One more thing...

344
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:25,231
I don't want to leave any paper trails.
The money has to be in cash.

345
00:38:25,320 --> 00:38:26,753
- Cash?
- Problem?

346
00:38:31,280 --> 00:38:33,430
- I suppose not.
- Good.

347
00:38:34,440 --> 00:38:36,908
I just want to thank you
for this opportunity.

348
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:38,638
- Likewise.
- Thank you.

349
00:39:58,240 --> 00:39:59,229
Work the spell.

350
00:40:14,560 --> 00:40:15,913
How're you doing?

351
00:40:18,120 --> 00:40:22,033
Save us both a little time.
I don't screw people I work with.

352
00:40:22,240 --> 00:40:23,229
No?

353
00:40:31,360 --> 00:40:33,237
Does that go for everyone?

354
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:35,998
Or just the troops?

355
00:40:38,560 --> 00:40:40,949
Do you want a little tip, Danny?
Some friendly advice?

356
00:40:41,040 --> 00:40:43,156
Yeah, that would be great.

357
00:40:43,240 --> 00:40:47,199
Watch and learn.
Stay out of things that don't concern you.

358
00:41:34,200 --> 00:41:38,591
- Your guys seem pretty tight.
- After a while, you get to be family.

359
00:41:41,200 --> 00:41:42,553
I say something funny?

360
00:41:42,640 --> 00:41:46,315
No. The whole family thing works for me.

361
00:41:47,720 --> 00:41:49,472
You play short cons, Danny.

362
00:41:49,560 --> 00:41:53,348
Emptying people's wage packets on
a Friday night. You wouldn't understand.

363
00:41:58,680 --> 00:42:01,240
- Can I make a criticism?
- Okay.

364
00:42:03,400 --> 00:42:05,072
£100,000?

365
00:42:06,600 --> 00:42:09,034
We can take this guy
for a lot more than that.

366
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:10,997
That's the bit you don't get.

367
00:42:11,680 --> 00:42:13,352
It's not just about the money.

368
00:42:13,440 --> 00:42:15,237
- Since when?
- Since always.

369
00:42:15,360 --> 00:42:17,874
You don't get that, you don't get anything.

370
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:23,554
My old man was as straight as they come.

371
00:42:24,720 --> 00:42:27,871
Never wanted much
and life didn't disappoint him.

372
00:42:28,440 --> 00:42:30,510
He worked, ate, slept.

373
00:42:30,600 --> 00:42:33,160
Paid his taxes on time, toed the line.

374
00:42:33,840 --> 00:42:37,435
The only dream he had was retiring at 60.

375
00:42:40,600 --> 00:42:44,991
Dropped dead in the Post Office
three days before his 60th birthday.

376
00:42:46,440 --> 00:42:49,591
He was paying his phone bill.

377
00:42:52,560 --> 00:42:54,755
- I'm sorry.
- Yeah. Me, too.

378
00:42:58,360 --> 00:43:01,238
You want to be regular people? Do you?

379
00:43:01,560 --> 00:43:05,189
Go do that. They're crying out
for foot soldiers down there.

380
00:43:05,640 --> 00:43:09,599
Go and have your life run by bosses,
bank managers, and politicians.

381
00:43:09,760 --> 00:43:12,228
But if you want to be a grifter...

382
00:43:13,040 --> 00:43:16,828
don't have anything in your life
you can't walk away from in a second.

383
00:43:17,440 --> 00:43:20,273
Lovers, friends, even your own mother.

384
00:43:25,080 --> 00:43:26,593
What about your wife?

385
00:43:29,360 --> 00:43:31,157
I heard what happened.

386
00:43:32,120 --> 00:43:35,715
She wanted me to be something I wasn't.

387
00:43:36,400 --> 00:43:38,868
And I was desperate not to disappoint her.

388
00:43:40,080 --> 00:43:41,672
Recipe for disaster.

389
00:43:41,880 --> 00:43:43,711
If you were still together...

390
00:43:43,800 --> 00:43:47,110
you're telling me you could
just walk away from her?

391
00:43:49,640 --> 00:43:51,312
First rule, Danny:

392
00:43:51,720 --> 00:43:53,631
Always look after number one.

393
00:43:55,000 --> 00:43:57,389
If you don't, no one else will.

394
00:44:01,640 --> 00:44:03,392
I'll try and remember that.

395
00:44:03,680 --> 00:44:06,114
Go home, Danny. Get some sleep.

396
00:44:27,800 --> 00:44:29,153
Danny Blue.

397
00:44:43,800 --> 00:44:45,711
The kid did well today.

398
00:44:47,920 --> 00:44:49,399
Reminds me of you.

399
00:44:50,640 --> 00:44:51,914
Worryingly.

400
00:44:53,440 --> 00:44:54,953
Okay, come on.

401
00:44:55,960 --> 00:44:57,473
How did you find him?

402
00:44:57,800 --> 00:45:00,678
- Am I that transparent?
- You taught me well.

403
00:45:01,440 --> 00:45:03,874
I heard about him. He's good.

404
00:45:04,600 --> 00:45:06,431
Wants to be the best.

405
00:45:07,840 --> 00:45:09,592
That sound familiar?

406
00:45:10,200 --> 00:45:12,760
I told Eddie to point him in your direction.

407
00:45:12,920 --> 00:45:14,876
I'm all but done anyway.

408
00:45:16,720 --> 00:45:19,712
- You going to miss it?
- We're all trying to get out.

409
00:45:19,800 --> 00:45:22,951
You would, too, if you could
think of something else to do.

410
00:45:27,880 --> 00:45:29,199
All right, boys.

411
00:45:40,800 --> 00:45:43,155
You know what I love about roulette?

412
00:45:43,960 --> 00:45:47,919
That glorious 30 seconds
while the wheel is still spinning.

413
00:45:54,400 --> 00:45:55,913
<i>Christopher Rawlings.</i>

414
00:45:58,160 --> 00:45:59,957
The horseracing scam.

415
00:46:01,440 --> 00:46:04,159
His wife found out
he'd lost their life savings.

416
00:46:05,040 --> 00:46:07,190
He's ready to press charges.

417
00:46:08,120 --> 00:46:10,076
You ever been to prison, Danny?

418
00:46:14,120 --> 00:46:16,714
- Shouldn't you be taping this?
- That's Plan B.

419
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:23,190
- Want to hear Plan A?
- Surprise me.

420
00:46:23,280 --> 00:46:26,989
We've already got enough to send
everyone down. No way around that.

421
00:46:27,080 --> 00:46:29,753
But bearing in mind who the mark is,
the powers that be...

422
00:46:29,840 --> 00:46:31,558
would rather not have a lengthy trial.

423
00:46:31,640 --> 00:46:34,598
They think the details
might be embarrassing.

424
00:46:35,000 --> 00:46:39,630
What they need is a watertight case
so overwhelming, everyone pleads guilty.

425
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:43,993
We'll rush the sentencing through
and the whole thing blows over.

426
00:46:45,400 --> 00:46:47,197
Sounds like you've got it all worked out.

427
00:46:47,280 --> 00:46:48,474
We have.

428
00:46:49,760 --> 00:46:53,514
We know you came into the game late.
So you get a lifeline.

429
00:46:53,920 --> 00:46:56,753
- Which is?
- Just do what you've been doing.

430
00:46:57,080 --> 00:47:00,356
After the arrests, we take you
to a safe house, you make a statement.

431
00:47:00,440 --> 00:47:02,635
Once the team knows
we've got your evidence...

432
00:47:02,720 --> 00:47:05,678
they'll know they can't do anything
but plead guilty.

433
00:47:05,760 --> 00:47:08,638
In return, you walk away into the sunset.

434
00:47:11,040 --> 00:47:12,632
What if I don't?

435
00:47:12,720 --> 00:47:15,109
Like I said, you go down
with the rest of them...

436
00:47:15,200 --> 00:47:18,192
and we'll add the Rawlings job
for good measure.

437
00:47:18,280 --> 00:47:22,558
All you have to do is go back,
pretend we never had this conversation.

438
00:47:36,880 --> 00:47:39,633
Mickey and the others are
going to prison whatever you do.

439
00:47:39,720 --> 00:47:44,157
The only choice you have to make
is whether you go with them or not.

440
00:47:44,240 --> 00:47:45,639
Be smart, Danny.

441
00:47:46,600 --> 00:47:48,352
Look after number one.

442
00:48:03,640 --> 00:48:06,279
- Where's the kid?
- He'll be here.

443
00:48:06,960 --> 00:48:08,951
Opening night nerves.

444
00:48:18,440 --> 00:48:20,112
Williams is on his way up.

445
00:48:59,680 --> 00:49:00,795
Gentlemen.

446
00:49:06,360 --> 00:49:07,475
You're late.

447
00:49:08,640 --> 00:49:10,756
- I'm sorry...
- Doesn't matter. There's no time.

448
00:49:10,840 --> 00:49:12,319
Let's get this done.

449
00:49:55,640 --> 00:49:57,835
- Where's Redford?
- He'll be here.

450
00:49:57,920 --> 00:50:00,195
I thought we were all
going to do this together.

451
00:50:00,280 --> 00:50:03,272
If there's a problem,
we can call the whole thing off.

452
00:50:25,640 --> 00:50:26,993
Here he is.

453
00:50:37,880 --> 00:50:39,199
Look, Mickey...

454
00:50:40,720 --> 00:50:43,188
Armed police!
Everybody stay exactly where you are!

455
00:50:43,280 --> 00:50:44,793
Put the case down!

456
00:50:45,400 --> 00:50:47,311
Everyone, hands on heads.

457
00:50:47,400 --> 00:50:48,674
What the hell is going on?

458
00:50:48,760 --> 00:50:51,035
You've been caught up
in a confidence trick, sir.

459
00:50:51,120 --> 00:50:53,588
These men are planning
to swindle you out of your money.

460
00:50:53,680 --> 00:50:55,557
There must be some mistake.

461
00:50:57,680 --> 00:50:59,910
All right, Danny. You can go.

462
00:51:02,440 --> 00:51:03,589
Judas.

463
00:51:04,880 --> 00:51:07,075
Listen, this is not how it looks.

464
00:51:08,160 --> 00:51:09,309
Get the cases.

465
00:51:19,560 --> 00:51:21,073
Don't do it, Michael.

466
00:51:21,400 --> 00:51:22,958
Final lesson, Danny.

467
00:51:25,040 --> 00:51:26,234
I mean it.

468
00:51:45,680 --> 00:51:47,671
- Oh, God!
- Get him out of here.

469
00:51:50,040 --> 00:51:51,268
Bastard!

470
00:51:53,200 --> 00:51:56,272
- We need a statement.
- I have my position to think of.

471
00:51:56,360 --> 00:51:59,477
- We understand that, sir.
- Just give me my money and I'll go.

472
00:51:59,560 --> 00:52:03,951
- We can't do that, it's evidence.
- Sod the money. Let me out of here.

473
00:52:04,040 --> 00:52:06,793
I can't be implicated in something
like this. It'll ruin me.

474
00:52:06,880 --> 00:52:09,678
If we have enough evidence,
we can get guilty pleas.

475
00:52:09,760 --> 00:52:12,399
- It won't go to court.
- Am I under arrest?

476
00:52:12,480 --> 00:52:15,313
- We need your statement.
- Am I under arrest?

477
00:52:17,680 --> 00:52:18,954
No, sir.

478
00:52:21,800 --> 00:52:23,472
Get out of my way.

479
00:52:25,800 --> 00:52:27,153
Let him go.

480
00:52:35,200 --> 00:52:37,430
Let's hope the boss can do his stuff.

481
00:52:39,200 --> 00:52:40,713
Sorry about the head.

482
00:53:02,080 --> 00:53:05,709
- You killed him.
- I saved your life, Danny.

483
00:53:07,400 --> 00:53:08,719
Everyone's in custody.

484
00:53:08,800 --> 00:53:11,189
We'll get you checked out
on the way to the station.

485
00:53:11,280 --> 00:53:14,352
First, time to keep your end of the bargain.

486
00:53:15,520 --> 00:53:17,954
Be sure not to miss anything out.

487
00:53:21,360 --> 00:53:22,918
I'm not a grass.

488
00:53:24,040 --> 00:53:25,473
Be smart, Danny.

489
00:53:26,080 --> 00:53:28,548
All you can do now is lose.

490
00:53:29,840 --> 00:53:31,273
Then I lose.

491
00:53:33,680 --> 00:53:36,752
I'd sooner that than hook up
with a murdering scumbag like you.

492
00:53:36,840 --> 00:53:40,355
- Stone fired a gun at you.
- He thought I'd betrayed him.

493
00:53:41,720 --> 00:53:44,792
You do this, or I'll see you get five years.

494
00:53:46,000 --> 00:53:47,353
Last chance.

495
00:53:50,520 --> 00:53:51,919
Go screw yourself.

496
00:53:57,720 --> 00:53:59,950
Okay, he's all yours.

497
00:54:15,160 --> 00:54:17,196
I knew you'd be okay, kid.

498
00:54:25,720 --> 00:54:28,029
- I don't get it.
- You're all right.

499
00:54:29,600 --> 00:54:30,999
Congratulations.

500
00:54:42,160 --> 00:54:44,674
<i>Sarge, the prisoners you asked for
are gone.</i>

501
00:54:44,760 --> 00:54:46,716
What do you mean, they've gone?

502
00:54:47,200 --> 00:54:48,679
Who signed them out?

503
00:54:49,120 --> 00:54:51,714
<i>It says here D.I. DePalma.</i>

504
00:55:20,080 --> 00:55:21,593
Don't do it, Michael.

505
00:55:22,080 --> 00:55:23,638
Final lesson, Danny.

506
00:55:25,240 --> 00:55:26,514
I mean it.

507
00:55:40,640 --> 00:55:42,278
I got something for you.

508
00:56:01,360 --> 00:56:03,920
If you don't know,
find me someone who does.

509
00:56:04,000 --> 00:56:06,275
I wanna know
who sent DePalma over here.

510
00:56:06,360 --> 00:56:09,318
Who gave him his orders,
who his superior officer is.

511
00:56:13,880 --> 00:56:18,192
No, you will not phone me back.
I wanna speak to someone there now.

512
00:56:33,080 --> 00:56:35,116
Danny Blue. Neil Cooper.

513
00:56:35,640 --> 00:56:39,792
This man defines the term "con artist. "

514
00:56:39,960 --> 00:56:42,793
I'm just passing through. Glad I could help.

515
00:56:44,040 --> 00:56:46,634
Hang on a minute. You shot at me.

516
00:56:46,720 --> 00:56:48,915
Yeah, but I missed, remember?

517
00:56:49,840 --> 00:56:51,910
You're telling me we're home free?

518
00:57:02,080 --> 00:57:05,072
<i>Absolutely. Unless the police
would like to go to court...</i>

519
00:57:05,160 --> 00:57:08,277
<i>and explain how the investigation
was led by one of the accused.</i>

520
00:57:12,800 --> 00:57:14,631
It's all in the detail, kid.

521
00:57:18,400 --> 00:57:19,719
A toast.

522
00:57:22,800 --> 00:57:25,030
To Mickey Bricks' last score.

523
00:57:27,000 --> 00:57:28,638
The end of an era.

524
00:57:34,400 --> 00:57:37,870
The sweetest con of all
is to con another grifter.

525
00:57:38,280 --> 00:57:40,669
One who should know better.

526
00:57:44,760 --> 00:57:46,990
You mean you're not retiring?

527
00:57:47,120 --> 00:57:49,759
I had to say something
to get you all together.

528
00:57:49,840 --> 00:57:52,115
You little devil.

529
00:57:52,200 --> 00:57:53,553
That's brilliant.

530
00:57:57,120 --> 00:58:00,271
It's not all about the money, is it, Mickey?

531
00:58:00,360 --> 00:58:02,237
Here endeth the first lesson.

532
00:58:03,237 --> 00:58:13,237
Downloaded From www.AllSubs.org

